<!--
    FS25 Realistic Harvesting — Crop Throughput Configuration Antler22
    ==========================================================

    HOW THIS FILE WORKS 
    The mod derives a power-law throughput curve for each crop using two
    real-world anchor points taken from AEM combine class data:

        buPerHrMin  = what a CLASS 6  combine  (≈ 280 hp) achieves
        buPerHrMax  = what a CLASS 11 combine  (= 775 hp) achieves

    Both anchors are quoted in ADVERTISED horsepower — the same number the
    store shows and the same number the mod logs as "[RHM] HP detected".

    From those two numbers the mod calculates:
        throughput (kg/s)  =  A  *  hp ^ B

    where B and A are solved automatically so the curve passes exactly
    through both anchor points.  Every combine HP between 280 and 775 then
    falls on the right calibrated curve — small machines land near the min,
    large machines land near the max.

    NOTE ON B > 1 (throughput rising faster than horsepower)
    This is expected and correct.  A modern Class 11 is not merely "more
    horsepower" than a Class 6 — it also has a far larger cleaning shoe,
    more separation area, and a wider head, so it converts each horsepower
    into more bushels.  Corn runs roughly 7.9 bu/hr per hp at Class 6
    versus 12.9 at Class 11.

    Small grains are cleaning-shoe limited rather than power limited, so
    they sit much closer to B = 1.0 than corn does — currently wheat 1.08,
    barley 0.98, oat and sorghum 0.96.  What matters is that those four
    stay in the same band as each other: a machine fills the shoe the same
    way in all of them, so one small grain scaling very differently from
    its siblings means an anchor is wrong, not that the crop is special.
    Wheat sat at B = 0.72 until the max anchor was raised from 2,500 to
    3,600 bu/hr, which also had barley moving MORE mass per hour than
    wheat — backwards, since barley is bulkier and lighter.
    tests/simcoretest.lua case [1] locks both properties.

    WHAT TO TUNE
    Change buPerHrMin and buPerHrMax to match what your local crop and
    variety actually produces under good conditions.  You do NOT need to
    list every crop — any crop not listed here uses the mod's built-in
    defaults.

    FILE LOCATION
    This file ships in the mod folder and loads automatically.
    To override it, copy it to:
        <My Documents>/My Games/FarmingSimulator2025/
            modSettings/FS25_RealisticHarvesting/cropThroughput.xml
    The modSettings copy takes priority over the bundled one.

    BUSHEL WEIGHTS USED (USDA test weight)
    wheat / soybean / legume / potato : 60 lb/bu
    corn / sorghum                    : 56 lb/bu
    barley                            : 48 lb/bu
    canola (rapeseed)                 : 50 lb/bu
    rice                              : 45 lb/bu
    oat / cotton                      : 32 lb/bu
    sunflower                         : 25 lb/bu

    REAL-WORLD ANCHOR REFERENCES
    (ideal conditions, modern header, proper settings — these are PEAK
     figures; the mod runs at targetEngineLoad, default 95%, and moisture
     reduces it further, so in-field rates land below these numbers)
    Class 6  ≈ 280 hp  e.g. JD S670,    Case IH 7250, NH CR8.90
    Class 11 = 775 hp  i.e. Case IH AF11

                        Class 6      Class 11
    Wheat               1,200        3,600 bu/hr
    Barley              1,400        3,800 bu/hr
    Oat                 1,200        3,200 bu/hr
    Corn                2,200       10,000 bu/hr   (AF11, observed)
    Soybean               800        3,200 bu/hr
    Canola                700        1,800 bu/hr
    Sunflower             450        1,100 bu/hr
    Sorghum             1,200        3,200 bu/hr
    Rice                  800        2,000 bu/hr
    Rice (long grain)     800        2,000 bu/hr
    Peas (PEA)            800        2,600 bu/hr
    Lentil (LENTIL)       700        2,200 bu/hr
    Chickpea (CHICKPEA)   750        2,400 bu/hr

    PREDICTED MID-RANGE (computed from the defaults above, peak bu/hr)
                        400 hp       500 hp       600 hp
    Wheat               1,763        2,243        2,731 bu/hr
    Barley              1,984        2,470        2,953 bu/hr
    Corn                3,737        5,208        6,830 bu/hr
    Soybean             1,300        1,761        2,257 bu/hr
    Canola                975        1,199        1,420 bu/hr

    SPOT CHECK — Case IH 8250 (555 hp, the log's "HP detected" value)
    Corn peak 6,084 bu/hr; at 95% target load and ~1.13 grain moisture
    that works out to roughly 5,100 bu/hr in the field.

    ALTERNATIVE: SINGLE-POINT MODE (advanced)
    If you only want to set one reference point, use buPerHrRef alone.
    The mod will treat it as the 400 hp reference and use a fixed exponent
    of 0.75.  This is less accurate than the two-point form.
        <crop name="wheat" buPerHrRef="1800" />
    CROPS ANCHORED RELATIVE TO ANOTHER CROP  (added by the port; see the block at the end of this file)
    A crop without anchors of its own can be defined as a multiple of one that has them:
        <crop name="rye" like="wheat" factor="0.90" />
    factor is throughput by MASS relative to the reference crop at the SAME horsepower, so the
    derived crop keeps the reference's curve shape (its exponent) and follows it if you retune
    the reference. Add kind="forage" to take the reference from the forage anchors instead of
    the grain ones. A crop may only be "like" one that is anchored explicitly - no chains.

-->

<cropThroughput>

    <!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    MOISTURE SYSTEM INTEGRATION  (requires the FS25_MoistureSystem mod)
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    enableLoad   — wet crop raises effective engine load (harder to thresh).
    enableLoss   — wet crop raises grain losses on sieves / rotor.
    enableSpeed  — wet standing crop reduces the recommended-speed cap.

    Set any flag to "false" to disable that effect channel independently.

    GLOBAL SCALE FACTORS
      maxLoadFactor     (+30% at max moisture by default)
      maxLossFactor     (×2.0 losses at max moisture by default)
      maxSpeedReduction (−35% speed cap at max moisture by default)
      dryBonusFactor    (5% load/loss bonus when below target moisture)

    PER-CROP THRESHOLDS  (USDA standard harvest moisture targets)
      target = ideal harvest moisture % — penalties start above this
      max    = practical ceiling % — factors are capped here
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    -->
    <moistureSettings
        enableLoad="true"
        enableLoss="true"
        enableSpeed="true"
        maxLoadFactor="1.30"
        maxLossFactor="2.00"
        maxSpeedReduction="0.35"
        dryBonusFactor="0.05">

        <!-- Small grains -->
        <crop name="wheat"      target="13.5" max="22.0" />
        <crop name="barley"     target="13.5" max="20.0" />
        <crop name="oat"        target="13.0" max="20.0" />
        <crop name="rye"        target="13.5" max="20.0" />
        <crop name="spelt"      target="13.5" max="20.0" />
        <crop name="triticale"  target="13.5" max="20.0" />
        <crop name="millet"     target="13.0" max="18.0" />  <!-- small-seeded; shatters above 18% -->

        <!-- Coarse grains -->
        <crop name="corn"       target="15.5" max="35.0" />  <!-- US farmers harvest to 30-35% routinely -->
        <crop name="sorghum"    target="14.0" max="28.0" />

        <!-- Oilseeds -->
        <crop name="soybean"    target="13.0" max="22.0" />
        <crop name="canola"     target="9.0"  max="20.0" />
        <crop name="sunflower"  target="9.0"  max="20.0" />

        <!-- Rice (harvested wet; dried post-field) -->
        <crop name="rice"       target="18.0" max="26.0" />  <!-- US target 18-20%; avoid above 25% -->

        <!-- Pulses -->
        <crop name="pea"        target="13.0" max="18.0" />
        <crop name="lentil"     target="13.0" max="16.0" />  <!-- very fragile; shatters above 16% -->
        <crop name="chickpea"   target="13.0" max="16.0" />  <!-- cracks badly above 16% -->

        <!-- Specialty -->
        <crop name="cotton"     target="8.0"  max="14.0" />

    </moistureSettings>

    <!-- ── Grain crops ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->

    <crop name="wheat"
          buPerHrMin="1200"
          buPerHrMax="3600" />

    <crop name="barley"
          buPerHrMin="1400"
          buPerHrMax="3800" />

    <crop name="oat"
          buPerHrMin="1200"
          buPerHrMax="3200" />

    <crop name="corn"
          buPerHrMin="2200"
          buPerHrMax="10000" />

    <crop name="sorghum"
          buPerHrMin="1200"
          buPerHrMax="3200" />

    <crop name="rice"
          buPerHrMin="800"
          buPerHrMax="2000" />

    <!-- Long-grain rice — same envelope as rough rice; listed separately so it can be
         tuned independently. FS25 spells this fill type riceLongGrain (no underscores). -->
    <crop name="riceLongGrain"
          buPerHrMin="800"
          buPerHrMax="2000" />

    <!-- ── Oilseed and specialty crops ──────────────────────────────────── -->

    <crop name="soybean"
          buPerHrMin="800"
          buPerHrMax="3200" />

    <crop name="canola"
          buPerHrMin="700"
          buPerHrMax="1800" />

    <crop name="sunflower"
          buPerHrMin="450"
          buPerHrMax="1100" />

    <!-- ── Pulse crops (FS25 keys: PEA, LENTIL, CHICKPEA) ──────────────── -->

    <crop name="pea"
          buPerHrMin="800"
          buPerHrMax="2600" />

    <crop name="lentil"
          buPerHrMin="700"
          buPerHrMax="2200" />

    <crop name="chickpea"
          buPerHrMin="750"
          buPerHrMax="2400" />

    <!-- ── Forage crops ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         HP anchors: tPerHrMin @ 400 hp (small/mid forage harvester)
                     tPerHrMax @ 950 hp (CLAAS Jaguar 990 / JD 9900 class)

         Values are in US SHORT TONS per hour (1 US short ton = 2000 lbs = 907 kg).
         The mod converts to kg/s internally. FS25 HUD always displays metric t/hr —
         to see what the HUD will show, multiply your value by 0.907.
             e.g. 455 US t/hr × 0.907 ≈ 413 metric t/hr on the HUD at 100% load

         Tune tPerHrMin / tPerHrMax to set the throughput envelope for each crop.
         Check the mod log for "[RHM] BasePerfMass:" to verify HP detection at startup.

                        400 hp              950 hp
         Corn silage    200 US t/hr         455 US t/hr   (reference — factor 1.00)
         Alfalfa        169 US t/hr         380 US t/hr   (factor 1.20 — tougher per kg)
         Grass (fresh)  150 US t/hr         338 US t/hr   (factor 1.35 — highest resistance)
         Dry grass      184 US t/hr         415 US t/hr   (factor 1.10)
         Clover         169 US t/hr         380 US t/hr   (factor 1.20)
         Triticale sil  225 US t/hr         507 US t/hr   (factor 0.90 — easier per kg)
         Sorghum sil    238 US t/hr         537 US t/hr   (factor 0.85)
         Sugarcane      290 US t/hr         652 US t/hr   (factor 0.70)
         Mint           184 US t/hr         415 US t/hr   (factor 1.10)
         Miscanthus     140 US t/hr         315 US t/hr   (factor 1.43 — very tough, fibrous dry stalks)
    -->

    <crop name="maize"
          tPerHrMin="200"
          tPerHrMax="455" />

    <crop name="corn"
          tPerHrMin="200"
          tPerHrMax="455" />

    <crop name="alfalfa"
          tPerHrMin="169"
          tPerHrMax="380" />

    <crop name="grass"
          tPerHrMin="150"
          tPerHrMax="338" />

    <crop name="drygrass"
          tPerHrMin="184"
          tPerHrMax="415" />

    <crop name="clover"
          tPerHrMin="169"
          tPerHrMax="380" />

    <crop name="triticale"
          tPerHrMin="225"
          tPerHrMax="507" />

    <crop name="sorghum"
          tPerHrMin="238"
          tPerHrMax="537" />

    <crop name="sugarcane"
          tPerHrMin="290"
          tPerHrMax="652" />

    <crop name="mint"
          tPerHrMin="184"
          tPerHrMax="415" />

    <crop name="soybean"
          tPerHrMin="160"
          tPerHrMax="360" />

    <crop name="pintobean"
          tPerHrMin="155"
          tPerHrMax="350" />

    <crop name="straw"
          tPerHrMin="165"
          tPerHrMax="370" />

    <crop name="miscanthus"
          tPerHrMin="140"
          tPerHrMax="315" />

    <crop name="woodchips"
          tPerHrMin="125"
          tPerHrMax="280" />

    <!--
    â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•
    CROPS ANCHORED RELATIVE TO ANOTHER CROP
    â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•â•
    Upstream's crop list is wider than the table above. A crop with no anchor falls back to
    upstream's own per-crop constant, and for the crops that DO have anchors those constants
    disagree with them by a factor of two or three - so an unanchored crop would run on a
    different scale from its neighbours (rye out-producing wheat, for instance).

    These entries close that gap. They are judgment calls tied to relationships already present
    in the table above, NOT measurements: capacity is limited by the cleaning shoe, so what
    matters is seed size and density and how much straw or other material comes through per
    kilogram of grain. Edit freely; the numbers are meant to be tuned.

    factor = mass throughput relative to the reference crop, at the same horsepower.
    -->

    <!-- Cereals - reference: wheat -->
    <crop name="rye"       like="wheat" factor="0.90" />   <!-- tall, tough straw: more material per kg of grain -->
    <crop name="triticale" like="wheat" factor="0.95" />   <!-- wheat-like, slightly more straw -->
    <crop name="spelt"     like="wheat" factor="0.80" />   <!-- hulled: bulky spikelets, heavy chaff load -->
    <crop name="millet"    like="wheat" factor="0.55" />   <!-- small round seed, fragile panicle -->
    <crop name="buckwheat" like="wheat" factor="0.50" />   <!-- delicate triangular seed that shatters; run slowly -->

    <!-- Small oilseeds and seed crops - reference: canola -->
    <crop name="mustard"    like="canola" factor="1.00" /> <!-- near-identical crop to canola -->
    <crop name="linseed"    like="canola" factor="0.85" /> <!-- small smooth seed; strong stems wrap on rotors and belts (also matches "flax") -->
    <crop name="safflower"  like="canola" factor="0.85" /> <!-- spiny heads; between canola and sunflower -->
    <crop name="poppy"      like="canola" factor="0.60" /> <!-- tiny seed in capsules; run slowly to hold losses down -->
    <crop name="hemp"       like="canola" factor="0.55" /> <!-- fibrous stalk wraps on rotors -->
    <crop name="grass_seed" like="canola" factor="0.30" /> <!-- light, chaff-like seed; strongly shoe-limited -->
    <crop name="clover"     like="canola" factor="0.35" /> <!-- clover seed crop (the forage "clover" below is a separate entry) -->

    <!-- Field beans - reference: soybean -->
    <crop name="beans" like="soybean" factor="1.00" />
    <crop name="bean"  like="soybean" factor="1.00" />

    <!-- Forage names upstream recognises but the table above does not list -->
    <crop name="poplar" like="woodchips" factor="1.00" kind="forage" />   <!-- short-rotation wood chipping -->
    <crop name="meadow" like="grass"     factor="1.00" kind="forage" />
    <crop name="silage" like="maize"     factor="1.00" kind="forage" />
    <crop name="gps"    like="maize"     factor="1.00" kind="forage" />   <!-- whole-crop silage -->
    <crop name="hay"    like="drygrass"  factor="1.00" kind="forage" />

</cropThroughput>
